notes on EyeSys files, RA, XX, PU, HDR 
RA == ?Radii of rings in EyeSys terms, increasing in magnitude from 40's to 400's with the EyeSys XX files being the sagittal curvature given in diopters 700's because R=(n-1)/D

notes on OPD-III Nidek files

from the manual and brochure:
33 vertical, 39 horizontal (6 extra partial horizontal rings horizontally)
11,880 points and more
Axial, Instantaneous, "Refractive", Elevation
33 x 360 = 11880

They are similar to EyeSys dat files.

The binary files may simply be compressed; they appear to be a 30-50% smaller than the uncompressed versions & the opd-iii manual describes "compressed vs uncompressed database"
However, if so, not very good nonstandard compression, for example AR*.DAT files ASCII are about 25Kbytes, binary versions are 14Kybes but using gzip on ASCII gives 3.7Kbytes

There are only 23 mires in the uncompressed files; the manual says the number of rings is adjustable up to 33
Nidek RA seems to be 7.xx curvatures (corresponding to EyeSys XX file) 
ED 0.40 to 4.x ? radii (corresponding to EyeSys RA file) 
HT* files seem to be elevation from 0.01- > 2.x
QV* files are Q values for CL fitting per the manual.

the compressed files appear to have 33 mires and 360 meridians (each degree)
They are calibration files, not patient data, stored as *.CAB files under Windows, extracted and all with *.DAT except for RP*.txt
RE*, PU*, PP*, EY* .DAT are all *.PNG image files, EY* are rings. There are 16 rings in an image seen
RP*.txt viewed in mc has hex data terminated with ASCII descriptors of cylinder, sphere, PD etc. look like summary data; line terminations are (0D 0A)
HD*. dat is almost certainly summary data and patient information similar to Export data function; line terminations are (0D 0A)

RA*. dat shows 360 data segments that are 138 bytes long each could be 17 x 8 bytes = 136 + 2 (0D 0A)
20 3 byte segments alternating with 19 4 bytes segments (ends in 3-byte section) then the + 2 (0D 0A)
39 3 byte segments in groups of 2 separated by EA or at the end E2

ED =?Eye Data
ED* .dat shows 360 data segments that are 138-141 bytes each could be 17 numbers x 8 bytes + +( 0-3 bytes ) + 2 (0D 0A) 
a 141 byte section has 17 3-byte sections alternating with 17 4 byte segments  then 5 4-byte almost identical sections then the + 2 (0D 0A)
                       39 3 bytes sections in groups of two separated by E2, E3, E4, E5, E6, E2 then in groups of 1 sep by D2
a 140 byte section has 17 3-byte sections alternating with 17 4 byte segments  then 4 4-byte almost identical sections and a terminal 3-byte section then + 2 (0D 0A)
                       39 3 bytes sections in groups of two separated by E2, E3, E4, E5, E6, E2 then in groups of 1 sep by D2 and a terminal 3-byte section
a 139 byte section has 20 3 byte segments alternating with 20 4 bytes segments then the + 2 (0D 0A)
                       39 3 bytes sections in groups of two separated by E2, E3, E4, E5, E6, E2 then in groups of 1 sep by D2                     
a 138 byte section has 20 3 byte segments alternating with 19 4 bytes segments = 39 total then the + 2 (0D 0A)
                       39 3 byte segments in groups of 2 separated by EA or at the end E2
                       39 alternating 3 and 4 byte groups starting with either AC24/23 or EAC2 until last seven with 2C22 or E2C2
                          is A==10 bits of data vs 2==2 bits of data?
AR==aberrometry? autorefraction?
PR, PE  == pupil radius? / pupil?

AR*. dat shows 361 data segments the first is a short segment 13 bytes, then 360 x 39-41 bytes each  = (11)37 + (0-2 bytes) + 2 bytes (0D 0A)
? Automated objective refraction (dynamic skiascopy) 2,520 points (7 x 360)
PR*.dat shows 361 data segments the first is a long segment 9 bytes, then 360 x 5 bytes each  = (7)3 + 2 bytes (0D 0A)
PE*.dat shows 361 data segments the first is a long segment 9 bytes, then 360 x 5 bytes each  = (7)3 + 2 bytes (0D 0A)


Misc notes
EyeSys EY*.DAT file is not an image
PentaCam U12 file looks like it has binary data separated by blocks of zero, possibly readable.
